tinguish mutually exclusive alternative paths from conditional branches: `switch` must strictly be used for selecting among categorical alternatives (e.g., 'Choose between A, B, or C'), while `if`/`elseif` must be used for conditional evaluations (e.g., 'If X is available, do Y, else do Z'); explicitly forbid substituting one for the other. (7) Do not flatten nested conditional logic (e.g., a condition within a branch of another condition) into a single-level if/elseif/else or switch/case structure; use nested if constructs instead. (8) Do not insert 'stop' nodes for conditional outcomes unless the requirement explicitly states the process terminates; instead, merge the branch back into the main flow. (9) Do not wrap unconditional statements, facts, or declarative actions in conditional logic (if/else or switch/case) unless the requirement text explicitly specifies a condition, guard, or alternative trigger for that action. (10) Do not duplicate conditional checks across different branches (e.g., checking the same threshold in both the 'yes' and 'no' paths of a prior check). (11) Do not insert `if`/`else` or `switch`/`case` constructs where the requirement describes a simple sequential flow; unconditional actions must not be wrapped in conditional logic.\n"
